Course.start({"num":5,"unit":1,"slug":"m05-simplifying","minutes":35,"calculator":"not needed","title":"Simplifying Expressions","subtitle":"The distributive property and combining like terms","lede":"Simplifying means rewriting an expression so it's shorter but means exactly the same thing. It's the move you'll make in almost every problem for the rest of the year.","recap":"In {{7x - 4y + 3}}, the terms are 7x, −4y and 3; 7 and −4 are coefficients; 3 is the constant. To evaluate, substitute values in parentheses.","goals":["Use the distributive property, including with negatives","Recognize like terms and combine them","Simplify expressions by distributing first, then combining like terms"],"context":{"title":"The human computer","place":"Imperial College, London · June 18, 1980","text":"On **June 18, 1980**, two 13-digit numbers were picked at random by a computer at Imperial College London:\n\n> 7,686,369,774,870 × 2,465,099,745,779\n\n**Shakuntala Devi**, who grew up in Bangalore, India, and never finished school, gave the 26-digit answer in **28 seconds**, without paper. The *Guinness Book of Records* listed it as a world record.\n\nDevi didn't just memorize. She broke big problems into easy pieces, the same idea you'll use this week. Want to do **7 × 98** in your head? Think of 98 as 100 − 2:\n\n> 7 × 98 = 7 × (100 − 2) = 700 − 14 = 686\n\nThat move, multiplying a number by each part of a group, is called the **distributive property**. It's how mental-math champions work, and it's the most-used tool in algebra.","ask":"How would you do 6 × 51 in your head? Try splitting 51 into two easy pieces."},"learn":[{"title":"The distributive property","text":"To multiply a number by a group, multiply it by **every term** in the group:\n\n> {{a(b + c) = ab + ac}}\n\nThink of area: a rectangle that's 3 wide and {{x + 4}} long has area {{3x + 12}}.","fig":"area","after":"**Distributing a negative** changes the sign of every term inside: {{-3(x - 4) = -3x + 12}}, because {{(-3)(-4) = 12}}. A minus sign in front of parentheses is like distributing −1: {{-(2x - 5) = -2x + 5}}.","worked":[{"problem":"{{5(2x - 3)}}","steps":[["{{5(2x) + 5(-3)}}","Multiply 5 by each term."],["{{10x - 15}}"]]},{"problem":"{{-4(x - 6)}}","steps":[["{{(-4)(x) + (-4)(-6)}}","Multiply −4 by each term, signs included."],["{{-4x + 24}}","Negative times negative is positive."]]}]},{"title":"Like terms","text":"**Like terms** have exactly the same variable part, including exponents. Only like terms can be combined.","table":{"head":["Like terms","Not like terms","Why not?"],"rows":[["{{3x}} and {{-7x}}","{{3x}} and {{3y}}","different variables"],["{{x^2}} and {{4x^2}}","{{x^2}} and {{x}}","different exponents"],["{{5}} and {{-2}}","{{5}} and {{5x}}","one has no variable"]]},"after":"To **combine** like terms, add their coefficients and keep the variable part: {{3x + 5x = 8x}}, {{4y - y = 3y}} (remember {{y = 1y}}), {{2x^2 - 6x^2 = -4x^2}}.\n\nIt's like counting objects: 3 apples + 5 apples = 8 apples, but 3 apples + 5 bananas can't be written as one amount.","check":{"q":"Simplify {{6x + 2 - x + 5}}.","choices":[["{{5x + 7}}",true,"6x − x = 5x and 2 + 5 = 7."],["{{12x}}",false,"x terms and constants can't be combined with each other."],["{{6x + 6}}",false,"Don't forget the −x: it combines with 6x."]]}},{"title":"Simplify: distribute, then combine","text":"To simplify an expression fully:\n1. **Distribute** to get rid of parentheses.\n2. **Combine like terms.** It helps to underline or circle each kind of term.\n\nWatch out for subtraction in front of a group: {{5x - (2x - 7)}} means you subtract **every** term in the group.","worked":[{"problem":"{{4(x + 2) - 3x + 1}}","steps":[["{{4x + 8 - 3x + 1}}","Distribute the 4."],["{{(4x - 3x) + (8 + 1)}}","Group like terms."],["{{x + 9}}"]]},{"problem":"{{5x - (2x - 7)}}","steps":[["{{5x - 2x + 7}}","The minus sign changes the sign of both terms in the group."],["{{3x + 7}}"]]}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"sort","title":"Like 3x or not?","intro":"Which terms could be combined with {{3x}}?","cats":["Like 3x","Not like 3x"],"items":[["{{-x}}",0,"−x has the same variable part, x."],["{{3x^2}}",1,"x² is not the same as x."],["{{0.5x}}",0,"Decimals are fine as coefficients."],["{{3}}",1,"A constant has no variable."],["{{7xy}}",1,"xy is a different variable part."],["{{3y}}",1,"Different variable."],["{{12x}}",0,"Same variable part."],["{{x/2}}",0,"{{x/2}} means {{(1/2)x}}."]],"hints":["Ignore the coefficients. Look only at the letters and exponents."]},{"id":"t2","type":"solve","title":"Distribute","pick":6,"intro":"Multiply out the parentheses. Type answers like **4x + 20**.","items":[{"q":"{{4(x + 5)}}","a":"4x + 20","from":"4(x+5)","how":["{{4(x) + 4(5)}}","**4x + 20**"]},{"q":"{{3(2x - 1)}}","a":"6x - 3","from":"3(2x-1)","how":["{{3(2x) + 3(-1)}}","**6x − 3**"]},{"q":"{{-2(x + 7)}}","a":"-2x - 14","from":"-2(x+7)","how":["{{(-2)(x) + (-2)(7)}}","**−2x − 14**"]},{"q":"{{-5(3 - x)}}","a":"-15 + 5x","from":"-5(3-x)","how":["{{(-5)(3) + (-5)(-x)}}","**−15 + 5x** (or 5x − 15)"]},{"q":"{{-(4x - 9)}}","a":"-4x + 9","from":"-(4x-9)","how":["The minus flips each sign: **−4x + 9**"]},{"q":"{{x(x + 3)}}","a":"x^2 + 3x","from":"x(x+3)","how":["{{x * x + x * 3}}","**x² + 3x**"]},{"q":"{{1/2(8x - 6)}}","a":"4x - 3","from":"(1/2)(8x-6)","how":["Half of 8x is 4x, half of −6 is −3.","**4x − 3**"]},{"q":"{{(y - 2)6}}","a":"6y - 12","from":"6(y-2)","how":["The number can come after the group. Same idea: **6y − 12**"]}],"hints":["Multiply the outside number by **every** term inside.","Keep each term's sign: {{-2 * 7 = -14}}, {{-5 * (-x) = 5x}}."]},{"id":"t3","type":"solve","title":"Combine like terms","pick":6,"items":[{"q":"{{3x + 5x}}","a":"8x","from":"3x+5x","how":["3 + 5 = 8: **8x**"]},{"q":"{{9y - y}}","a":"8y","from":"9y-y","how":["{{y}} is {{1y}}: 9 − 1 = 8, so **8y**"]},{"q":"{{4a + 3 - a + 6}}","a":"3a + 9","from":"4a+3-a+6","how":["a terms: 4a − a = 3a. Constants: 3 + 6 = 9.","**3a + 9**"]},{"q":"{{-2x + 7 + 5x - 10}}","a":"3x - 3","from":"-2x+7+5x-10","how":["−2x + 5x = 3x. 7 − 10 = −3.","**3x − 3**"]},{"q":"{{x^2 + 4x - 3x^2 + x}}","a":"-2x^2 + 5x","from":"x^2+4x-3x^2+x","how":["x² terms: 1 − 3 = −2. x terms: 4 + 1 = 5.","**−2x² + 5x**"]},{"q":"{{6m - 4n - 2m + n}}","a":"4m - 3n","from":"6m-4n-2m+n","how":["m terms: 6m − 2m = 4m. n terms: −4n + n = −3n.","**4m − 3n**"]},{"q":"{{0.5x + 1.2 - 2x}}","a":"-1.5x + 1.2","from":"0.5x+1.2-2x","how":["0.5 − 2 = −1.5.","**−1.5x + 1.2**"]},{"q":"{{8 - 3k - 8}}","a":"-3k","from":"8-3k-8","how":["8 − 8 = 0.","**−3k**"]}],"hints":["Circle the x terms, box the constants. Combine each group separately.","The sign in front of a term belongs to it."]},{"id":"t4","type":"mistake","title":"Spot the slip","intro":"Tap the **first line** with a mistake. Then type the correct simplified answer.","items":[{"q":"Simplify {{-3(x - 5)}}","lines":["{{(-3)(x) + (-3)(-5)}}","{{-3x - 15}}"],"bad":1,"why":"The −3 times the −5 is +15, not −15: {{-3x + 15}}.","a":"-3x + 15","from":"-3(x-5)"},{"q":"Simplify {{2(x + 4) + 3}}","lines":["{{2x + 4 + 3}}","{{2x + 7}}"],"bad":0,"why":"The 2 must multiply the 4 too: {{2x + 8 + 3 = 2x + 11}}.","a":"2x + 11","from":"2(x+4)+3"},{"q":"Simplify {{7x - (3x + 2)}}","lines":["{{7x - 3x + 2}}","{{4x + 2}}"],"bad":0,"why":"Subtracting the group means subtracting the 2 as well: {{7x - 3x - 2 = 4x - 2}}.","a":"4x - 2","from":"7x-(3x+2)"},{"q":"Simplify {{4x + 3x^2}}","lines":["{{4x + 3x^2 = (4 + 3)x^(1 + 2)}}","{{= 7x^3}}"],"bad":0,"why":"x and x² aren't like terms, so this is already simplified: {{3x^2 + 4x}}.","a":"3x^2 + 4x","from":"4x+3x^2"}],"hints":["Check every sign when a negative is distributed.","Only like terms combine."]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Simplify fully","pick":5,"intro":"Distribute first, then combine like terms.","items":[{"q":"{{3(x + 2) + 4x}}","a":"7x + 6","from":"3(x+2)+4x","how":["{{3x + 6 + 4x}}","**7x + 6**"]},{"q":"{{5(2y - 1) - 3y}}","a":"7y - 5","from":"5(2y-1)-3y","how":["{{10y - 5 - 3y}}","**7y − 5**"]},{"q":"{{2(x + 3) + 3(x - 1)}}","a":"5x + 3","from":"2(x+3)+3(x-1)","how":["{{2x + 6 + 3x - 3}}","**5x + 3**"]},{"q":"{{8a - 2(a + 4)}}","a":"6a - 8","from":"8a-2(a+4)","how":["{{8a - 2a - 8}}","**6a − 8**"]},{"q":"{{4 - (x - 6)}}","a":"10 - x","from":"4-(x-6)","how":["{{4 - x + 6}}","**10 − x** (or −x + 10)"]},{"q":"{{-3(2n - 1) + 6n}}","a":"3","from":"-3(2n-1)+6n","how":["{{-6n + 3 + 6n}}","The n terms cancel: **3**"]},{"q":"{{x(x - 2) + 5x}}","a":"x^2 + 3x","from":"x(x-2)+5x","how":["{{x^2 - 2x + 5x}}","**x² + 3x**"]}],"hints":["Step 1: get rid of every set of parentheses.","Step 2: combine x terms with x terms and numbers with numbers."]},{"id":"t6","type":"match","title":"Mental math like Devi","left":"Problem","right":"Easy way","intro":"Match each problem with the distributive trick that makes it easy.","pairs":[["{{7 * 98}}","{{7(100 - 2) = 700 - 14}}"],["{{6 * 51}}","{{6(50 + 1) = 300 + 6}}"],["{{4 * 199}}","{{4(200 - 1) = 800 - 4}}"],["{{9 * 23}}","{{9(20 + 3) = 180 + 27}}"],["{{12 * 105}}","{{12(100 + 5) = 1200 + 60}}"]],"hints":["Look for the number close to a round number."]}],"build":{"heading":"Explain a simplification","prompt":"Simplify {{3(2x - 4) - (x - 5)}}, **one step per line**. After your steps, write **two sentences**: one about the distributive property and one about like terms, explaining what you did.","model":"3(2x - 4) - (x - 5)\n= 6x - 12 - x + 5\n= 5x - 7\nFirst I used the distributive property: I multiplied 3 by 2x and by −4, and the minus sign in front of (x − 5) changed both of its signs. Then I combined like terms: 6x − x = 5x and −12 + 5 = −7.","placeholder":"3(2x - 4) - (x - 5)\n= …","reqs":[{"t":"Show at least 2 steps with equal signs","re":"=","min":2},{"t":"Get the right answer: 5x − 7","re":"5\\s*x\\s*[-−]\\s*7"},{"t":"Mention the *distributive* property","re":"\\bdistribut"},{"t":"Mention *like terms*","re":"\\blike terms?\\b"}]},"reflect":{"q":"Shakuntala Devi said anyone could get better at math by practicing the right way. What's one study habit that actually works for you in math? What's one you want to try this year?","placeholder":"One habit that works for me is…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Simplify these","items":[{"q":"{{2[3(x - 1) - (x + 4)]}}","a":"4x - 14","from":"2*(3*(x-1)-(x+4))","how":["Inside the brackets: {{3x - 3 - x - 4 = 2x - 7}}","{{2(2x - 7) = 4x - 14}}"]},{"q":"{{1/2(4x - 6) - (x - 3)}}","a":"x","from":"(1/2)(4x-6)-(x-3)","how":["{{2x - 3 - x + 3}}","**x**"]},{"q":"{{-(-2x + 5) + 3(x - 1)}}","a":"5x - 8","from":"-(-2x+5)+3(x-1)","how":["{{2x - 5 + 3x - 3}}","**5x − 8**"]},{"q":"{{x(x + 4) - 2x(x - 1)}}","a":"-x^2 + 6x","from":"x(x+4)-2x(x-1)","how":["{{x^2 + 4x - 2x^2 + 2x}}","**−x² + 6x**"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"solve","title":"Perimeter puzzles","fig":"rect","items":[{"q":"A rectangle is {{x + 3}} long and {{2x - 1}} wide. Write a simplified expression for its **perimeter** (the distance around).","a":"6x + 4","from":"2(x+3)+2(2x-1)","how":["Perimeter = 2(length) + 2(width) = {{2(x + 3) + 2(2x - 1)}}","{{2x + 6 + 4x - 2 = 6x + 4}}"]},{"q":"If the perimeter is 40, what does {{6x + 4}} tell you? Find {{x}}. (Hint: what number times 6, plus 4, makes 40?)","a":"6","pre":"x =","eq":"6x + 4 = 40","how":["{{6x + 4 = 40}}, so {{6x = 36}}.","**x = 6**. You'll learn to solve equations like this in Unit 2."]}]}],"figs":{"area":{"x":[0,7],"y":[0,3],"w":300,"pad":26,"bare":true,"segs":[[0,0,7,0,0],[0,3,7,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0],[3,0,3,3,0],[7,0,7,3,0]],"texts":[[1.5,1.5,"3x"],[5,1.5,"12"],[1.5,3.4,"x"],[5,3.4,"4"],[-0.5,1.5,"3"]],"alt":"A rectangle 3 tall, split into an x part with area 3x and a 4 part with area 12"},"rect":{"x":[0,6],"y":[0,3],"w":240,"pad":22,"bare":true,"segs":[[0,0,6,0,0],[0,3,6,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0],[6,0,6,3,0]],"texts":[[3,3.35,"x + 3"],[6.2,1.5,"2x − 1"]],"alt":"A rectangle with length x + 3 and width 2x − 1"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** Shakuntala Devi also wrote books to help children enjoy numbers, including *Figuring: The Joy of Numbers* (1977). She believed math anxiety came from bad teaching, not from students.","unitTitle":"Number Sense Bootcamp","code":"MDg5MS1FVFVCSVJUU0lE","star":"NzUxMy1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m04-variables-expressions.html","next":"m06-one-step-equations.html"});
